Part 3 — One resampling core, three seams
🔴 This is the single most important structural decision in the whole document, and every plan you were shown missed it.

You do not write three ReSTIRs. Weighted reservoir sampling, the temporal rule, the spatial rule, the M-cap, the MIS weight, the pairwise combination — all identical across T0, T2, T3. What differs is exactly three functions. Specialize those and nothing else.

Seam	T0	T2	T3
ClassifyOcclusion	Illuminant depth-atlas lookup, then screen-space depth-pyramid march for contact scale	RayQueryKHR with terminate-on-first-hit	Same, or HitObject in the T3 pipeline
ResolveTargetDensity	Unshadowed p̂ — reflectance × emitted radiance × geometry	Same, plus visibility reuse from the retained reservoir	Full path contribution, unified over direct and indirect
ProjectSample	Reprojection only, Jacobian = 1	Single-vertex reconnection, closed-form solid-angle Jacobian	Reconnection · random replay · hybrid shift, with extent-based reconnection criteria
Everything else is one body of code, specialized in Slang by a constant, compiled to four SPIR-V permutations. That collapses your maintenance surface from three renderers to one renderer and nine small functions.

📐 The reservoir, shared in a .slang.h header so host and device agree:

Copy//  💾 32 bytes for the direct reservoir, 48 for the indirect. Both 16-byte aligned for coalesced access.
struct DirectReservoir
{
    uint32_t    ChosenEmitter;         // [-]   - Identity<Emitter>, packed; 0xFFFFFFFF when vacant
    uint32_t    ChosenPosition;        // [-]   - barycentric pair, 2×16-bit unorm on the emitter's face
    float       WeightSum;             // [-]   - Σ wᵢ over every candidate this reservoir has seen
    float       ContributionWeight;    // [-]   - W = WeightSum / (M · p̂(chosen)); 0 when p̂ = 0
    uint16_t    CandidateCount;        // [-]   - M, saturating at ResamplingCeiling
    uint16_t    RotationAge;           // [-]   - rotations survived; feeds the duplication test
    uint32_t    ReconstructionSeed;    // [-]   - reproduces the candidate stream for validation
};
Note the spellings: no Sample collision with 64's SampleIntegrator, no history, no flag, no count as a bare word. M is CandidateCount; W is ContributionWeight; p̂ appears in comments as a real Unicode glyph per SKILL-Formatting §5.

The core update, which every tier calls unchanged:

Copy/// 🧩 Accumulates one candidate into a reservoir by weighted reservoir sampling; replaces on ξ < wᵢ/WeightSum.
/// in    ActiveReservoir   [-]  updated in place; vacant reservoirs are a legal input
/// in    CandidateWeight   [-]  wᵢ = p̂(candidate) / p(candidate); zero candidates are absorbed, not skipped
/// in    ξ                 [-]  uniform on [0,1), from the decorrelated sequence
/// out   Replaced          [-]  the candidate became the chosen one
/// err   never refuses; a non-finite weight is clamped and reported through 86
/// cost  ✔️
/// pre   CandidateWeight ≥ 0 and finite
/// post  ActiveReservoir.CandidateCount increased by exactly one
/// tag   device, shared, nonallocating
bool Accumulate(inout DirectReservoir ActiveReservoir, EmitterCandidate Candidate, float CandidateWeight, float ξ);
⚠️ Zero-weight candidates are absorbed, not skipped. Skipping them corrupts M and biases every downstream MIS weight. This is the most common ReSTIR bug and it produces an image that looks merely "a bit bright", so it survives review.

