Part 6 — T0 in detail: the cheapest resampling that is still resampling
For hardware with no ray query at all. Biased, leaky, and honest about both.

Direct. Full weighted reservoir sampling over the illuminant population, unchanged from Bitterli. The only change is ClassifyOcclusion. Instead of a shadow ray:

① A single depth atlas holds the N most significant illuminants, N ≈ 8–16, tiled, refreshed round-robin at one to four tiles per rotation. ② p̂ during resampling is unshadowed — reflectance × emitted radiance × geometry term. No visibility at all in the target function. This is what makes it cheap; you are resampling a distribution that ignores occlusion. ③ Visibility is applied once, at shade time, from the atlas, plus a short screen-space march down the depth-extremum pyramid for contact-scale occlusion the atlas resolution cannot express. ④ 🔴 If shade-time visibility is zero, kill the reservoir — set ContributionWeight to zero and CandidateCount to one. This is Bitterli's visibility reuse, and without it the temporal chain keeps re-selecting an occluded emitter forever and you get a stable, confident, wrong shadow.

Cost: one atlas lookup and roughly eight to sixteen pyramid steps per pixel. No traversal, no acceleration structure, no VK_KHR_*. Runs on anything with compute. This is structurally what MegaLights does on its non-hardware-ray-traced path, and Arm's mobile writeup is the best public description of the tradeoff.

Indirect. Screen-space probes on a sparse lattice — one probe per 8×8 or 16×16 pixels, each with a small spherical parameterization, say 8×8 directions. Each probe's directions are traced by marching the depth-extremum pyramid. On a march miss, fall back to 94's world hash rather than to the sky, and inject reflective-depth samples from the same illuminant atlas into that hash each rotation so the hash has content the screen never saw. Reservoirs live per probe direction, resampled temporally against the probe's reprojected position and spatially against adjacent probes. Interpolate to pixels with depth-and-normal weights.

Named failure modes, so you recognize them rather than debug them. Off-screen indirect is as good as the hash and no better — thin. Contact shadows from anything off-screen are absent. Grazing-angle reflections degrade to the hash, which is blurry. Light leaks through geometry thinner than the hash cell. These are the same failures Lumen's software path has, for the same structural reason, and the correct response is to ship T0 as the compatibility tier and never as the reference.

Part 7 — T3 in detail: the RTX tier
Everything T2 has, plus:

Unified reservoirs. Direct and indirect share one reservoir species rather than two, per the 2026 paper. This is the change that saves the most bandwidth and it is also the most invasive — it is why T3 is Phase 7 and not Phase 4.

Reciprocal neighbour selection. Halves spatial reuse cost by making the neighbour relation symmetric so each pair is resampled once instead of twice.

Extent-based reconnection criteria. Replaces the fixed distance-and-roughness thresholds with a criterion derived from the ray differential extent. This is what makes shift mappings stop producing fireflies at glossy chains.

Duplication indices. Tracks which reservoirs descend from a common ancestor and suppresses the resulting spatiotemporal correlation. (Map is banned; Index is both correct and the right suffix.)

Reordering, Ada and later only. VK_EXT_ray_tracing_invocation_reorder, and it requires the ray-tracing-pipeline path. Turing and Ampere do not have the hardware; the extension is not merely slow there, it is absent. Gate on the extension, never on RTX-in-the-device-name.

Opacity micromaps, Ada and later. VK_KHR_opacity_micromap, driver 596.50, May 2026. Only pays for itself with alpha-tested foliage.

Denoiser. Swap 98 for NRD ReBLUR on diffuse and ReLAX on specular, or DLSS Ray Reconstruction where available. 98 stays compiled for T0–T2.

